---
import ContentShell from '../../layouts/ContentShell.astro'
import { allJobs, loadAll, stats } from '../../data/load'
import { SITE_NAME } from '../../data/seo'
import { url } from '../../data/slugs'
import { GUIDES, articleLd, guideUrl, otherGuides } from '../../data/guides'

const guide = GUIDES.find((g) => g.slug === 'how-to-read-a-startup-job-posting')!

const jobs = await allJobs()
const companies = await loadAll()
const s = await stats()

const remote = jobs.filter((j) => j.remote_type === 'remote').length
const hybrid = jobs.filter((j) => j.remote_type === 'hybrid').length
const atsNames = [...new Set(companies.filter((c) => c.ats).map((c) => c.ats))].sort()

const description = guide.blurb
---

<ContentShell
  title={`${guide.title} | ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description={description}
  path={guideUrl(guide.slug)}
  h1={guide.h1}
  lede={description}
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' }, { name: guide.nav, href: guideUrl(guide.slug) }]}
  jsonLd={[articleLd(guide, Astro.site!, SITE_NAME)]}
  narrow
>
  <p>
    A startup job posting is usually written by the person you would report to, not by an HR
    template, which makes it shorter and more honest than a services-company JD — and also less
    standardised. The same words mean different things at different companies. Here is how to read
    one without ruling yourself out of a role you could get, or walking into one you should not.
  </p>

  <h2>The title tells you less than the number suggests</h2>
  <p>
    Seniority labels are not consistent across companies. As a rough map: SDE-1, Associate or
    Junior is first year or two; SDE-2 or Engineer II is a few years in; Senior or Lead means
    owning a surface with little supervision; Staff or Principal is a senior individual
    contributor, usually many years deep. But one company's "Senior" is another's "SDE-2", and a
    twelve-person startup hands out titles differently from a three-hundred-person one. Treat the
    title as a hint about scope, not a gate. If the responsibilities read like work you can do,
    the number in the title is not the thing to stop on.
  </p>

  <h2>What the experience range here is derived from</h2>
  <p>
    Most Indian postings state seniority in words — "SDE-1", "3+ years", "strong fundamentals" —
    rather than a structured field. The experience range shown on a job page here is read out of
    the title and description by pattern matching; the employer did not fill in a number for us.
    That means it is a filtering shortcut and it is wrong sometimes. The real claim is in the
    posting itself, so read that before you decide you do not qualify. The method and its limits
    are in <a href={url.about()}>how this works</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>"Hybrid" and "remote" mean something specific in Pune</h2>
  <p>
    On this site {hybrid} open {hybrid === 1 ? 'role names' : 'roles name'} hybrid work and{' '}
    {remote} {remote === 1 ? 'names' : 'name'} remote, out of {s.jobs}. Hybrid at a Pune startup
    usually means two to four days in the office, in whichever area the company sits — which is
    why the <a href="/guides/where-pune-startup-jobs-are/">area a company is in</a> still decides
    your commute even for a "hybrid" role. Remote is filtered strictly here: a role only counts as
    remote if it names India and does not name another metro, because a bare "Remote" on a job
    board is usually US-remote. If a posting says remote but the team is in one city, expect that
    to mean hybrid in practice, and ask.
  </p>

  <h2>Must-haves versus the wish list</h2>
  <p>
    The requirements block is two lists wearing one heading. A few items are genuine must-haves —
    the language the codebase is in, a specific framework the team lives in, sometimes a degree
    for a visa or client reason. The rest is a wish list of everything that would be nice. Nobody
    matches all of it. If you meet the must-haves and a fair share of the rest, you are a real
    candidate; apply and let the team decide. Waiting until you match every line is how the role
    closes without you in it.
  </p>

  <h2>Red flags worth walking away from</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Any request for money.</strong> A registration fee, a training deposit, payment for
      equipment or a background check — no legitimate employer asks a candidate for these. It is
      the clearest scam signal there is. More on this in <a href="/terms/">terms of use</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A role with no shape.</strong> If after reading it you cannot say what you would do
      in the first month, the company has not decided either, and you will absorb that confusion.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Rockstar", "ninja", "work hard play hard", "like a family".</strong> Not
      disqualifying on their own, but a posting leaning on them instead of describing the work is
      often covering for long hours or a thin plan.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An unpaid "assignment" that is really free work.</strong> A short take-home is
      normal. A multi-day build that looks a lot like a feature they need is not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pressure to decide immediately.</strong> A real offer survives you sleeping on it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a startup posting leaves out</h2>
  <p class="muted">
    Almost always, salary. Indian startups rarely publish a band, which is why no page on this
    site shows one — a number we invented would be worse than the silence. That is a question for
    the first real conversation, and asking it early is normal, not pushy.
  </p>

  <h2>And where you actually apply</h2>
  <p>
    The apply button goes to the employer's own system —{' '}
    {atsNames.length ? atsNames.join(', ') : 'their ATS'} — never to this site. What each of those
    costs you in time, and which companies use which, is the next guide:{' '}
    <a href="/guides/which-ats-a-company-uses/">which ATS a company uses</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top:2em">Other guides</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {otherGuides(guide.slug).map((g) => (
      <a class="tile" href={guideUrl(g.slug)}>
        <h3>{g.nav}</h3>
        <span class="meta">{g.blurb}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
</ContentShell>
